"""Are the lines I just wrote tested? One command, from an unbuilt tree to one number.

    Tools/Scripts/webkit-coverage svg/                # measure my change, running svg/
    Tools/Scripts/webkit-coverage --dry-run           # what would it do, and what should I run?
    Tools/Scripts/webkit-coverage --full-suite        # all 106,172 layout tests

Until now this took three things a developer had to know independently, none of which was checked
and none of which was documented in the tree: build-webkit --coverage,
ENABLE_USER_SCRIPT_SANDBOXING=NO, and a WEBKIT_OUTPUTDIR of its own. Forget the first and the
run completes, exits 0 and collects nothing -- with the only warning arriving after the run,
which for the layout suite is hours later.

So this does five things, in this order, and prints what it is doing at each step:

1.  Resolves one build tree and says where it came from. webkit-build-directory applies a
    last-built tiebreaker and will hand out the wrong tree, so nothing here asks it.
2.  Pre-flights that the binaries are actually instrumented, which takes 0.34 s for all nine
    products and is the single biggest win in the tool. Also checks the things known to cost
    hours: an orphaned httpd or pywebsocket3 from a killed run, a held profile-directory lock,
    and an ambiguous llvm-cov.
3.  Builds if it has to, forcing the required settings and printing each one with its reason.
4.  Resolves a test scope and prints it. A suggestion is never applied automatically -- see
    below.
5.  Reports patch coverage of the lines the change added, scoped with --sources, and prints one
    number and one path. The uncovered added lines are the product; the percentage is not.

The scope is a SUGGESTION, always. With tests named on the command line this runs those tests
and marks every number in the resulting report as a lower bound; with no tests named it prints
what it would suggest and stops, because the alternatives are to start a two-hour run nobody
asked for or to silently restrict one, and both are worse than asking. --full-suite is the
explicit way to say "all of it".

Why a subset changes the numbers: adding tests can only turn a line from uncovered to covered,
so in a subset run a covered line is exact and an uncovered line is unknown. Every percentage
from a scoped run is therefore written `>= 41.30%`, the report directory and the trace get a
-selective infix, --fail-under-lines refuses to gate on it, and it cannot be compared against a
full-suite baseline. --fail-under-patch does work, because it is sound in the useful direction:
it can raise a false alarm and it cannot grant a false pass.
"""

import logging
import optparse
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import time

from webkitpy.common.host import Host
from webkitpy.coverage_requirements import (
    BUILD_OUTPUT_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE, DEFAULT_BUILD_DIRECTORY_NAME, FATAL, INSTRUMENTED_PRODUCTS,
    NOTE, WARNING, build_directory_from_environment, conflicting_build_settings,
    coverage_build_command, instrumentation_findings, missing_build_settings, normalize_build_root,
    orphaned_server_findings, product_name, survey_products, toolchain_findings)
from webkitpy.coverage_scope import CoverageScope
from webkitpy.coverage_test_scope import layout_test_names, suggest_scope
from webkitpy.coverage_thresholds import COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE
from webkitpy.llvm_profile_utils import (
    COVERAGE_PROFILE_DIRECTORY, CoverageProfileDirectoryInUse,
    acquire_coverage_profile_directory_lock, release_coverage_profile_directory_lock)
from webkitpy.port import configuration_options, platform_options

_log = logging.getLogger('webkit-coverage')

# Distinct from 1 (the tooling broke) and from COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE (a gate fired). This one
# means "I need you to tell me what to run": there is nothing wrong, and nothing has been done.
NEEDS_A_SCOPE_EXIT_CODE = 3

# The refs to try for "since what", in order, when --since is not given. A branch this checkout
# does not have must not become a git error message about a ref the developer never typed.
_DEFAULT_SINCE_REFS = ('main', 'origin/main')

_EPILOG = """
EXIT CODES
  0  the report was produced.
  1  something is wrong: an uninstrumented tree, a failed build, a broken test run.
  {gate}  a gate fired -- --fail-under-patch was not met.
  {scope}  no tests were named, so nothing has been run. The suggestion is above; run
     webkit-coverage again with a scope, or with --full-suite.

WHAT A SCOPED RUN CHANGES
  Adding tests can only turn a line from uncovered to covered, so in a run over part of the
  suite a covered line is exact and an uncovered line is unknown. A scoped run therefore
  produces a report whose every percentage is written >=, in a -selective directory, with a
  -selective trace, which --fail-under-lines refuses to gate on and compare-coverage-reports
  refuses to compare against a full-suite baseline.

  --fail-under-patch is the gate that still works, and it is sound but not complete: a line it
  calls covered really was executed, so it can raise a false alarm and it cannot grant a false
  pass. For a gate, that is the right direction.

THE SUGGESTION
  Name-based and advisory, from the 12 watchlist DEFINITIONS blocks that co-map Source/ to
  LayoutTests/, from directory-name alignment, and from the tests the change itself edits or
  rebaselines. It is never applied automatically, and it declines whenever it has no rule --
  which is most of the time, and correctly: the measured mean blast radius of a real change is
  86.1% of the suite, with p25 through p90 all at 100%.
""".format(gate=COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE, scope=NEEDS_A_SCOPE_EXIT_CODE)


class _PreformattedEpilog(optparse.IndentedHelpFormatter):
    """optparse rewraps the epilog into one blob, and the paragraph breaks carry meaning."""

    def format_epilog(self, epilog):
        return epilog or ''


def parse_args(args):
    parser = optparse.OptionParser(usage='%prog [options] [TEST ...]', epilog=_EPILOG,
                                   formatter=_PreformattedEpilog())
    parser.add_options(platform_options())
    parser.add_options(configuration_options())
    parser.add_option('--build-directory', metavar='DIR',
                      help='The coverage build tree. Either the tree or its configuration '
                           'directory; both are accepted. Defaults to ${} and then to '
                           '<checkout>/{}, which is deliberately not the ordinary build '
                           'directory: an instrumented WebCore is many times the size of an '
                           'uninstrumented one'.format(BUILD_OUTPUT_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE,
                                                       DEFAULT_BUILD_DIRECTORY_NAME))
    parser.add_option('--build', type='choice', choices=('auto', 'always', 'never'),
                      default='auto', metavar='WHEN',
                      help='auto (the default) builds only when the pre-flight says the tree '
                           'cannot produce coverage; always builds first; never refuses to '
                           'build and fails instead')
    parser.add_option('--build-argument', action='append', dest='build_arguments', default=[],
                      metavar='ARG',
                      help='Extra argument for build-webkit, after the required ones so it wins '
                           'a conflict (repeatable)')
    parser.add_option('--since', metavar='REF',
                      help='The change to measure, as a git ref: `main` means everything since '
                           'main including the working tree and untracked files, `main...HEAD` '
                           'only the commits. Defaults to {}'.format(' then '.join(
                               _DEFAULT_SINCE_REFS)))
    parser.add_option('--full-suite', action='store_true', default=False,
                      help='Run the whole suite. Required to run it, because a run nobody asked '
                           'for takes hours')
    parser.add_option('--api-tests', metavar='ARGS', nargs=1,
                      help='Also run the API tests, with ARGS passed to run-api-tests. Pass an '
                           'empty string for all of them. They get a coverage directory of '
                           'their own, so the report gets a column per suite')
    parser.add_option('--output-dir', metavar='DIR',
                      help='Where to write the report. Defaults to coverage-report under the '
                           'build directory, with a -selective infix for a scoped run')
    parser.add_option('--coverage-dir', metavar='DIR',
                      help='Where to collect raw profiles. Defaults to coverage-profiles under '
                           'the build directory')
    parser.add_option('--all-sources', action='store_true', default=False,
                      help='Report on the whole tree rather than only the files the change '
                           'touched. The scoped report is 8.6 MB and about 15 s; the whole-tree '
                           'one is 366 MB and 45 s, and answers a question this tool is not '
                           'asking')
    parser.add_option('--build-inventory', action='store_true', default=False,
                      help='Work out which files this configuration never compiled. Off here, '
                           'unlike in generate-coverage-report: it costs 17 s and its answer is '
                           'about the whole tree, not about the change')
    parser.add_option('--fail-under-patch', type='float', metavar='PERCENT',
                      help='Exit {} if fewer than PERCENT of the added lines that have coverage '
                           'data were executed. Sound but not complete on a scoped run: a false '
                           'alarm is possible and a false pass is not'.format(
                               COVERAGE_GATE_EXIT_CODE))
    parser.add_option('-n', '--dry-run', action='store_true', default=False,
                      help='Print the plan -- the tree, the pre-flight, the build command, the '
                           'change, the scope -- and do nothing')
    parser.add_option('-v', '--verbose', action='store_true', default=False, help='Verbose logging')

    options, tests = parser.parse_args(args)
    if options.full_suite and tests:
        parser.error('--full-suite means every test, so it cannot be combined with a test list')
    if options.fail_under_patch is not None and not 0 <= options.fail_under_patch <= 100:
        parser.error('--fail-under-patch is a percentage, so it has to be between 0 and 100')
    return options, tests


def heading(text):
    print('')
    print('== {} '.format(text) + '=' * max(0, 76 - len(text)))


def bullet(text, indent=2):
    for index, line in enumerate(_wrap(text, 78 - indent)):
        print(' ' * indent + ('* ' if index == 0 else '  ') + line)


def _wrap(text, width):
    words, lines, current = text.split(), [], ''
    for word in words:
        if current and len(current) + 1 + len(word) > width:
            lines.append(current)
            current = word
        else:
            current = (current + ' ' + word).strip()
    if current:
        lines.append(current)
    return lines or ['']


def report_findings(findings):
    """Print pre-flight findings and return True if any of them is fatal."""
    fatal = False
    for finding in findings:
        label = {FATAL: 'FATAL', WARNING: 'WARNING', NOTE: 'note'}[finding.severity]
        bullet('{}: {}'.format(label, finding.summary))
        bullet(finding.detail, indent=6)
        if finding.remedy:
            print('      fix: {}'.format(finding.remedy))
        fatal = fatal or finding.severity == FATAL
    return fatal


def resolve_build_root(options, checkout_root):
    """(root, where it came from). The one tree everything in this run will use.

    Absolute, because every command this tool prints should be one a developer can paste
    somewhere else, and because the harnesses are run with the checkout as their working
    directory rather than with the developer's.
    """
    if options.build_directory:
        root = normalize_build_root(os.path.abspath(options.build_directory),
                                    options.configuration)
        if root.note:
            bullet(root.note)
        return root.root, '--build-directory'
    from_environment = build_directory_from_environment()
    if from_environment:
        root = normalize_build_root(os.path.abspath(from_environment), options.configuration)
        if root.note:
            bullet(root.note)
        return root.root, '$' + BUILD_OUTPUT_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE
    return (os.path.join(checkout_root, DEFAULT_BUILD_DIRECTORY_NAME),
            'the default, since neither --build-directory nor ${} was given'.format(
                BUILD_OUTPUT_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE))


def build_command(options):
    """The build-webkit command line, and the lines explaining what was added to it."""
    added = missing_build_settings(options.build_arguments)
    conflicts = conflicting_build_settings(options.build_arguments)
    command = coverage_build_command(options.configuration,
                                     extra_arguments=options.build_arguments)
    explanations = []
    for setting in added:
        explanations.append('added {}: {}'.format(setting.argument, setting.why))
    for given, setting in conflicts:
        explanations.append('you passed {} and {} is required, and yours is last on the command '
                            'line so yours wins -- which will not work: {}'.format(
                                given, setting.argument, setting.why))
    return command, explanations


def default_since_ref(checkout_root):
    for ref in _DEFAULT_SINCE_REFS:
        completed = subprocess.run(['git', '-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', 'rev-parse', '--verify',
                                    '--quiet', ref], cwd=checkout_root, capture_output=True,
                                   text=True)
        if not completed.returncode:
            return ref
    return None


def changed_paths(checkout_root, ref):
    """(checkout-relative changed paths, the change's unified diff).

    core.fsmonitor is off, because it is on in this checkout and has been observed reporting a
    clean tree that was not clean. A scope resolved from a file list that silently
    omits the file the developer just edited is the failure this tool exists to prevent.
    """
    def git(*arguments):
        completed = subprocess.run(['git', '-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false', *arguments],
                                   cwd=checkout_root, capture_output=True, text=True)
        if completed.returncode:
            raise RuntimeError('git {} failed: {}'.format(arguments[0],
                                                          completed.stderr.strip()))
        return completed.stdout

    diff = git('diff', '--no-ext-diff', '--no-textconv', '--no-color', '-U0', ref)
    paths = set(git('diff', '--name-only', '--no-renames', ref).split())
    if '..' not in ref:
        paths |= set(git('ls-files', '--others', '--exclude-standard', '--full-name').split())
    return sorted(paths), diff


def print_paths(paths, limit=12, indent=6):
    for path in paths[:limit]:
        print(' ' * indent + path)
    if len(paths) > limit:
        print(' ' * indent + '... and {} more'.format(len(paths) - limit))


def print_declined(declined, limit=12):
    for entry in declined[:limit]:
        print('      {}: {}'.format(entry.path, entry.reason))
    if len(declined) > limit:
        print('      ... and {} more'.format(len(declined) - limit))


def print_scope_suggestion(suggestion, limit=20):
    if suggestion.empty:
        bullet('No suggestion. Every path in this change is one with no name-based rule, or one '
               'whose blast radius is the whole suite:')
        print_declined(suggestion.declined)
        bullet('So scope it yourself, or run the suite. That is the honest answer and not a '
               'gap in the tool: the measured mean blast radius of a real change is '
               '86.1% of the suite, with p25 through p90 all at 100%.')
        return
    tests = suggestion.tests
    bullet('Suggested scope, which is a suggestion and has not been applied ({} '
           'path(s)):'.format(len(tests)))
    print_paths(tests, limit=limit)
    if len(tests) > limit:
        bullet('That is more paths than are worth retyping, which is itself the answer: a '
               'change this wide is a change to run the suite for.')
    for rule, rule_tests in suggestion.rules().items():
        if not rule_tests:
            continue
        bullet('{} ({}):'.format(rule, len(rule_tests)), indent=4)
        print_paths(rule_tests, limit=6, indent=8)
    if not suggestion.complete:
        bullet('INCOMPLETE. {} of the changed paths have no suggestion, so these tests do not '
               'cover the whole change:'.format(len(suggestion.declined)))
        print_declined(suggestion.declined)


def source_scope_arguments(paths, checkout_root):
    """--sources arguments for the changed source files, so the report is about the change.

    The files themselves rather than their directories: the question is whether the lines this
    change added are tested, llvm-cov's --sources takes a file as happily as a directory, and
    measured against the same profile and binaries a scoped export is 8.6 MB where the whole-tree
    one is 751 MB.
    """
    from webkitpy.coverage_delta import SOURCE_EXTENSIONS

    arguments = []
    for path in paths:
        if not path.endswith(SOURCE_EXTENSIONS):
            continue
        absolute = os.path.join(checkout_root, path)
        if os.path.exists(absolute):
            arguments += ['--sources', absolute]
    return arguments


def run(command, overrides=None, dry_run=False, cwd=None):
    """Print a command exactly as it will run, then run it. Returns its exit code.

    The environment overrides are printed as a `VAR=value` prefix rather than being applied
    silently, so that every command this tool prints is one a developer can paste. WEBKIT_OUTPUTDIR
    is the whole point of that: it is one of the four things nobody knew they had to set, and a
    printed command that omits it would run against a different tree by hand than it does here.
    """
    overrides = overrides or {}
    prefix = ['{}={}'.format(name, overrides[name]) for name in sorted(overrides)]
    print('')
    print('+ {}'.format(' '.join(prefix + command)))
    if dry_run:
        print('  (--dry-run, so not run)')
        return 0
    sys.stdout.flush()
    environment = dict(os.environ)
    environment.update(overrides)
    return subprocess.call(command, env=environment, cwd=cwd)


def probe_profile_directory_lock():
    """[Finding] for a concurrent coverage run, checked by taking the lock and letting it go.

    Advisory, and deliberately not held: the harness acquires it for the run itself, and flock is
    per open file, so holding it here would make the harness's own acquisition fail. What this
    buys is failing in a second rather than after a build -- two concurrent runs destroy
    each other's profiles in both directions.
    """
    from webkitpy.coverage_requirements import Finding

    try:
        acquire_coverage_profile_directory_lock()
    except CoverageProfileDirectoryInUse as failure:
        return [Finding(FATAL, 'another coverage run is in flight on this machine', str(failure),
                        None)]
    release_coverage_profile_directory_lock()
    stale = [name for name in os.listdir(COVERAGE_PROFILE_DIRECTORY)
             if name.endswith('.profraw')] if os.path.isdir(COVERAGE_PROFILE_DIRECTORY) else []
    if stale:
        return [Finding(NOTE, '{} uncollected raw profile(s) in {}'.format(
            len(stale), COVERAGE_PROFILE_DIRECTORY),
            'Left by an interrupted run. The test run will delete them before it starts, '
            'because %Nm merges into an existing profile rather than replacing it, so keeping '
            'them would fold another run\'s counters into this one\'s.', None)]
    return []


def main(args):
    options, tests = parse_args(args)
    logging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG if options.verbose else logging.INFO,
                        format='%(levelname)s: %(message)s')

    host = Host()
    port = host.port_factory.get(options.platform, options)
    checkout_root = port.webkit_base()
    configuration = port.get_option('configuration') or 'Release'
    options.configuration = configuration

    heading('Build tree')
    build_root, provenance = resolve_build_root(options, checkout_root)
    build_path = os.path.join(build_root, configuration)
    bullet('{} ({}), from {}'.format(build_root, configuration, provenance))
    bullet('Everything below uses that one tree. webkit-build-directory is not consulted: it '
           'applies a last-built tiebreaker and will hand out a different tree than the one the '
           'tests ran against.')
    overrides = {BUILD_OUTPUT_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE: build_root}

    command, explanations = build_command(options)
    heading('Pre-flight')
    survey = survey_products(build_path)
    bullet('Read {} products in {:.2f} s: {} instrumented, {} uninstrumented, {} not '
           'built.'.format(len(survey.products), survey.seconds, len(survey.instrumented),
                           len(survey.uninstrumented), len(survey.missing)))
    findings = instrumentation_findings(survey, build_path, command)
    needs_build = any(finding.severity == FATAL for finding in findings)
    if report_findings(findings) and options.build == 'never':
        bullet('--build=never, so this is where it stops. The tree cannot produce coverage.')
        return 1
    for finding in toolchain_findings():
        report_findings([finding])

    heading('The change')
    ref = options.since or default_since_ref(checkout_root)
    if not ref:
        _log.error('Could not find %s in this checkout, so there is no default for --since. '
                   'Pass --since=REF.', ' or '.join(_DEFAULT_SINCE_REFS))
        return 1
    try:
        paths, diff = changed_paths(checkout_root, ref)
    except RuntimeError as error:
        _log.error('%s', error)
        return 1
    if not paths:
        bullet('Nothing has changed since {}, so there is no patch to measure. This will still '
               'produce a coverage report; it just will not be about anything in '
               'particular.'.format(ref))
    else:
        bullet('{} changed path(s) since {}.'.format(len(paths), ref))
        print_paths(paths)

    heading('Test scope')
    suggestion = suggest_scope(checkout_root, paths, diff)
    if tests:
        bullet('You named {}, so that is what will run. The suggestion below is printed for '
               'comparison only; nothing here narrows what you asked for.'.format(
                   ' '.join(tests)))
    print_scope_suggestion(suggestion)
    if not tests and not options.full_suite:
        bullet('No tests named, so nothing has been run. Give a scope -- for example '
               '`webkit-coverage {}` -- or pass --full-suite.'.format(
                   suggestion.tests[0] if suggestion.tests else 'svg/'))
        return NEEDS_A_SCOPE_EXIT_CODE

    # The API tests are part of the scope whenever they are named as a subset, and they are part
    # of the digest whenever they run at all. Without that, two runs of the same layout tests and
    # different API tests would carry the same test-name digest and would therefore be allowed to
    # compare against each other, which is exactly the hole the digest exists to close.
    api_argv, api_names = [], []
    if options.api_tests is not None:
        api_arguments = options.api_tests.split()
        api_argv = ['(api) ' + (' '.join(api_arguments) or 'all')]
        api_names = ['api:' + argument for argument in api_arguments] or ['api:all']

    scope = CoverageScope.full_suite()
    if tests or (options.api_tests is not None and options.api_tests.split()):
        started = time.time()
        selected = layout_test_names(port, tests) if tests else layout_test_names(port, [])
        in_suite = layout_test_names(port, [])
        if tests and not selected:
            _log.error('The harness\'s own finder finds no tests under %s, so a run would report '
                       '"Found 0 tests" and exit 0.', ' '.join(tests))
            return 1
        scope = CoverageScope.selective(list(tests) + api_argv, tests_run=len(selected),
                                       tests_in_suite=len(in_suite),
                                       test_names=selected + api_names, suite_name='layout')
        bullet('Counted the scope with the harness\'s own finder in {:.1f} s, so both numbers '
               'mean the same thing. The count is the layout suite\'s; a named API-test subset '
               'is in the scope and in its digest but not in these two '
               'numbers.'.format(time.time() - started))
        for line in scope.banner_lines():
            bullet(line)
    else:
        bullet('--full-suite, so every number in the report is exact rather than a lower bound.')
    if options.api_tests is None:
        bullet('The API tests are not being run, so a line only TestWebKitAPI reaches will be '
               'reported as uncovered. Pass --api-tests="" to run them too. The full-suite figures'
               ' are an API run and a layout run folded together.')

    output_dir = options.output_dir or os.path.join(build_root, 'coverage-report')
    coverage_dir = options.coverage_dir or os.path.join(build_root, 'coverage-profiles')
    report_dir = scope.directory(output_dir)

    for finding in probe_profile_directory_lock():
        if report_findings([finding]):
            return 1
    if not options.dry_run:
        for finding in orphaned_server_findings():
            report_findings([finding])

    if needs_build or options.build == 'always':
        heading('Build')
        if not needs_build:
            bullet('--build=always, so building even though the tree is already instrumented.')
        for explanation in explanations:
            bullet(explanation)
        bullet('Not silently corrected: those are the settings a coverage build needs, and the '
               'command below is the one to run by hand next time.')
        if run(command, overrides=overrides, dry_run=options.dry_run, cwd=checkout_root):
            _log.error('The build failed. Nothing has been run.')
            return 1
        if not options.dry_run:
            survey = survey_products(build_path)
            if report_findings(instrumentation_findings(survey, build_path, command)):
                _log.error('The build succeeded and the tree still cannot produce coverage.')
                return 1
    else:
        heading('Build')
        bullet('Not building: every required product under {} is already instrumented. Pass '
               '--build=always to build anyway.'.format(build_path))

    heading('Run')
    layout_command = [os.path.join('Tools', 'Scripts', 'run-webkit-tests'),
                      '--' + configuration.lower(), '--coverage',
                      '--coverage-dir=' + (coverage_dir + '-layout' if options.api_tests is not None
                                           else coverage_dir),
                      '--build-directory=' + build_root, *tests]
    if run(layout_command, overrides=overrides, dry_run=options.dry_run, cwd=checkout_root):
        # Not fatal. A test failure is a test failure, and the coverage of a run with failures is
        # still the coverage of that run -- the profiles are collected in a finally block exactly
        # so that an interrupted run still yields data.
        bullet('run-webkit-tests exited non-zero. Continuing: the profiles are collected even '
               'from a failed or interrupted run, so there is still a report to make.')
    if options.api_tests is not None:
        api_command = [os.path.join('Tools', 'Scripts', 'run-api-tests'),
                       '--' + configuration.lower(), '--coverage',
                       '--coverage-dir=' + coverage_dir + '-api',
                       '--build-directory=' + build_root]
        api_command += options.api_tests.split()
        if run(api_command, overrides=overrides, dry_run=options.dry_run, cwd=checkout_root):
            bullet('run-api-tests exited non-zero. Continuing, for the same reason.')

    heading('Report')
    scope_file = os.path.join(coverage_dir, 'test-scope.json')
    if not options.dry_run:
        os.makedirs(coverage_dir, exist_ok=True)
        scope.write(scope_file)
    report_command = [os.path.join('Tools', 'Scripts', 'generate-coverage-report'),
                      '--' + configuration.lower(), '--build-directory=' + build_root,
                      '--output-dir=' + output_dir, '--test-scope-file=' + scope_file]
    if options.api_tests is not None:
        report_command += ['--suite=layout:' + coverage_dir + '-layout',
                           '--suite=api:' + coverage_dir + '-api']
    else:
        report_command += ['--coverage-dir=' + coverage_dir]
    if not options.build_inventory:
        report_command.append('--no-build-inventory')
    if not options.all_sources:
        source_arguments = source_scope_arguments(paths, checkout_root)
        if source_arguments:
            bullet('Scoping the report to the {} changed source file(s) with --sources, which is '
                   'what makes it about the change rather than about the '
                   'project.'.format(len(source_arguments) // 2))
            report_command += source_arguments
        else:
            bullet('The change touches no C, C++ or Objective-C source file, so there is nothing '
                   'to scope the report to and it will describe the whole tree.')
    status = run(report_command, overrides=overrides, dry_run=options.dry_run,
                 cwd=checkout_root)
    if status:
        _log.error('generate-coverage-report failed, so there is nothing to measure the patch '
                   'against.')
        return 1

    trace = os.path.join(report_dir, scope.filename('coverage.lcov.gz'))
    patch_command = [os.path.join('Tools', 'Scripts', 'compare-coverage-reports'),
                     '--current=' + trace, '--git-diff=' + ref,
                     '--output-dir=' + report_dir]
    if options.fail_under_patch is not None:
        patch_command.append('--fail-under-patch={}'.format(options.fail_under_patch))
    status = run(patch_command, overrides=overrides, dry_run=options.dry_run,
                 cwd=checkout_root)

    heading('Result')
    if options.dry_run:
        bullet('--dry-run, so nothing above was run. The report would be at {}.'.format(
            os.path.join(report_dir, 'index.html')))
        return 0
    print(summarize(report_dir, scope))
    return status


def summarize(report_dir, scope):
    """One number and one path. The number is a count of lines, not a percentage.

    The percentage is in the report and in compare-coverage-reports' own output above. What a
    developer acts on is the list of added lines nothing executed, and its length is the one
    number worth putting last -- it is a count, so it is exact even on a scoped run, where the
    percentage is only a bound.
    """
    uncovered = None
    summary_path = os.path.join(report_dir, 'patch-summary.txt')
    try:
        with open(summary_path) as handle:
            for line in handle:
                if line.startswith('Patch coverage:'):
                    covered, _, total = line.split('(')[1].split(')')[0].partition(' of ')
                    uncovered = int(total.split()[0].replace(',', '')) - \
                        int(covered.replace(',', ''))
                    break
    except (OSError, IndexError, ValueError):
        pass

    lines = ['']
    if uncovered is None:
        lines.append('No patch summary was written, so there is no uncovered-line count. See '
                     'the report.')
    else:
        lines.append('{:,} uncovered added line(s){}'.format(
            uncovered, ' -- at most, since this was a selective run and an unexecuted line may '
                       'simply not have been asked' if scope.is_selective else ''))
        lines.append('  named individually in {}'.format(summary_path))
    lines.append('{}'.format(os.path.join(report_dir, 'index.html')))
    return '\n'.join(lines)


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
